From: Linexa Engineering <linexa@fem.linexa>
Date: 2026-08-25
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] execve: PROOT_GUEST_ENV_FILTER — scrub host-only
 variables from the tracee environment

Linexa F-8 fix (audit LINEXA_TERMINAL_RUNTIME_SOURCE_AUDIT.md §1.190/§16/§20).

## Root cause

PRoot passes its own environment to the tracee verbatim (verified
empirically against the vendored binary: `env` inside the guest is
byte-identical to the launcher's env). On Android launchers that
environment must carry LD_LIBRARY_PATH / PROOT_TMP_DIR / PROOT_LOADER /
TERMINFO for PRoot itself and its bundled binaries to work at all — and
those HOST paths then leak into every guest process, violating the
separation between the host/bootstrap execution environment and the
guest Linux environment. Upstream proot has no --env/--unsetenv options
in this lineage, which is why the leak was previously documented as
unfixable without a proot rebuild.

## The fix

A new opt-in, launcher-driven env scrub, applied at the ENTER side of
every execve translation (before the loader/QEMU machinery touches
envp):

    PROOT_GUEST_ENV_FILTER="NAME1,NAME2,..."

For every listed name, the variable is removed from the tracee's envp[]
when — and only when — its value is byte-identical to PRoot's own value
for that name. Value matching (not name-only matching) is what keeps
guest-authored values intact: a guest wrapper script exporting its own
LD_LIBRARY_PATH before exec'ing a binary keeps working (verified:
`LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/guest/own /bin/sh -c env` inside proot preserves the
guest value), while the copy inherited unchanged from the host launcher
is dropped. Names PRoot itself does not carry are skipped entirely.

Entry removal uses resize_array_of_xpointers(envp, index + 1, -1) — the
"remove slot index" form of PRoot's own array helper — plus
compare_xpointee_env-based lookup, so the mechanism is exactly the one
the QEMU path (ldso_env_passthru / rebuild_host_ldso_paths) already
exercises. Duplicated entries of a filtered name are removed in a loop.

The scrub is best-effort by design: an unfetchable envp surfaces the
fetch error, but a merely-unmatched variable is a no-op.

## Why opt-in (per invocation) rather than always-on

PRoot invocations that deliberately run HOST binaries as the tracee must
keep the host environment: Linexa's rootfs extractor runs its bundled
bionic `tar` under `proot --link2symlink`, and that tar needs
LD_LIBRARY_PATH to resolve its own shared libraries. The extractor
therefore does not set PROOT_GUEST_ENV_FILTER, while every guest-session
launch (PRootLauncher.buildLaunchSpec) does, listing exactly the
host-owned variables: LD_LIBRARY_PATH, PROOT_TMP_DIR, PROOT_LOADER,
TERMINFO, and PROOT_GUEST_ENV_FILTER itself (so the control variable
does not leak either).

## Verification (x86_64 build of the same source, real ptrace)

Filter set, env {A, LD_LIBRARY_PATH, B, C, PATH, PROOT_LOADER,
PROOT_TMP_DIR, TERMINFO, PROOT_GUEST_ENV_FILTER, HOME, TERM, LANG,
COLORTERM}:
    guest env = {A, B, C, PATH, HOME, TERM, LANG, COLORTERM}  — exactly
    the five host-owned variables removed, nothing else touched.
Filtered variable first (slot 0) and last (final real slot): removal
correct at every position; no blank entries left behind.
Guest-authored LD_LIBRARY_PATH: preserved.
Full L2S (F-1/F-2) regression battery re-run with the filter active:
unchanged (member/sentinel lstat, family lifecycle, dpkg -i over
relative-target families all still pass).

## Upstream status

Not applicable — an opt-in extension, not a defect fix; nothing
equivalent exists in termux/proot master as of 2026-08-25. Carried in
jniLibs/proot-build/ and compiled into the vendored libproot.so pair,
consistent with the existing patch practice.

Regression test: covered end-to-end by the Kotlin
PRootLauncherGuestEnvFilterTest / PRootExecNoninteractiveDebconfTest env
assertions (the fake proot records the full env of every launch) plus
the on-guest `env` checks above.

diff --git a/src/execve/enter.c b/src/execve/enter.c
--- a/src/execve/enter.c
+++ b/src/execve/enter.c
@@ -592,6 +592,123 @@
  * translate_load_*().  This function returns -errno if an error
  * occured, otherwise 0.
  */
+/**
+ * Linexa patch 0004 (F-8, 2026-08-25 audit): remove HOST-ONLY variables
+ * from the tracee's environment, so the guest sees a clean Linux
+ * environment instead of the launcher's plumbing.
+ *
+ * PRoot passes its own environment to the tracee verbatim; on Android
+ * launchers (Linexa, termux-style) that environment must carry
+ * LD_LIBRARY_PATH / PROOT_TMP_DIR / PROOT_LOADER / TERMINFO for PRoot and
+ * its bundled binaries to work at all — and those host paths then leak
+ * into every guest process. This build has no --env/--unsetenv options,
+ * so the scrub is driven by an environment variable read by PRoot itself:
+ *
+ *     PROOT_GUEST_ENV_FILTER="NAME1,NAME2,..."
+ *
+ * For every listed name, the variable is removed from the tracee's envp[]
+ * when — and only when — its value is byte-identical to PRoot's own value
+ * for that name. Value matching (not name-only matching) is what keeps
+ * guest-authored values intact: a guest wrapper script that exports its
+ * own LD_LIBRARY_PATH before exec'ing a binary keeps working, while the
+ * copy inherited unchanged from the host launcher is dropped. Names PRoot
+ * itself does not carry are skipped entirely.
+ *
+ * The filter is opt-in per invocation: PRoot invocations that deliberately
+ * run HOST binaries as the tracee (Linexa's rootfs extractor runs its
+ * bundled bionic `tar` under `proot --link2symlink`, and that tar needs
+ * LD_LIBRARY_PATH to link) simply do not set PROOT_GUEST_ENV_FILTER.
+ *
+ * This runs at the ENTER side of every execve translation, before the
+ * loader/QEMU machinery touches envp (which is also why it must never
+ * return a fatal error for a merely-unfilterable environment — the scrub
+ * is a best-effort hygiene step, not a correctness gate).
+ */
+/**
+ * Linexa patch 0004 helper: remove envp[@index] from @envp.
+ * resize_array_of_xpointers(envp, index + 1, -1) is the "remove slot
+ * index" form: the memmove shifts everything from index+1 down onto
+ * index, and the `index >= 1` assert inside resize() is satisfied by
+ * construction (index + 1 >= 1). Verified against the minimal case
+ * [A, LD_LIBRARY_PATH=x, B, C, ..., NULL]: removing slot 1 leaves
+ * [A, B, C, ..., NULL] byte-identical for the untouched entries.
+ */
+static int remove_env_entry(ArrayOfXPointers *envp, size_t index)
+{
+	return resize_array_of_xpointers(envp, index + 1, -1);
+}
+
+static int scrub_guest_env(Tracee *tracee)
+{
+	ArrayOfXPointers *envp;
+	char *filter;
+	char *name;
+	char *saveptr;
+	int status;
+
+	filter = getenv("PROOT_GUEST_ENV_FILTER");
+	if (filter == NULL || filter[0] == '\0')
+		return 0;
+
+	filter = strdup(filter);
+	if (filter == NULL)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	status = fetch_array_of_xpointers(tracee, &envp, SYSARG_3, 0);
+	if (status < 0) {
+		free(filter);
+		return status;
+	}
+
+	/* Environment variables should be compared with the "name"
+	 * part of the "name=value" string format.  */
+	envp->compare_xpointee = (compare_xpointee_t) compare_xpointee_env;
+
+	for (name = strtok_r(filter, ",", &saveptr);
+	     name != NULL;
+	     name = strtok_r(NULL, ",", &saveptr)) {
+		const char *host_value;
+		size_t name_length;
+
+		host_value = getenv(name);
+		if (host_value == NULL)
+			continue; /* not a host-carried variable: nothing leaked */
+
+		name_length = strlen(name);
+
+		while (1) {
+			char *entry;
+			size_t index;
+
+			status = find_xpointee(envp, name);
+			if (status < 0 || (size_t) status >= envp->length)
+				break; /* error or not found */
+
+			index = (size_t) status;
+			status = read_xpointee_as_string(envp, index, &entry);
+			if (status < 0)
+				break;
+
+			/* Remove only values inherited unchanged from the host. */
+			if (   strlen(entry) <= name_length
+			    || entry[name_length] != '='
+			    || strcmp(&entry[name_length + 1], host_value) != 0)
+				break;
+
+			status = remove_env_entry(envp, index);
+			if (status < 0)
+				break;
+		}
+	}
+
+	status = push_array_of_xpointers(envp, SYSARG_3);
+	free(filter);
+	if (status < 0)
+		return status;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
 int translate_execve_enter(Tracee *tracee)
 {
 	char user_path[PATH_MAX];
@@ -611,6 +728,13 @@
 		return 0;
 	}
 
+	/* Linexa patch 0004 (F-8): drop host-only variables from the
+	 * tracee's environment before anything else inspects or rewrites
+	 * envp. Best-effort by design — see scrub_guest_env(). */
+	status = scrub_guest_env(tracee);
+	if (status < 0)
+		return status;
+
 	status = get_sysarg_path(tracee, user_path, SYSARG_1);
 	if (status < 0)
 		return status;
